WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.480
How does Kruskal's algorithm work?

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.480
We will connect these eight

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.600
places as cheaply as possible.

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.040
Unlike Prim, we can build

5
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.060
several separate trees and join

6
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:11.620
them later.

7
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.960
Begin with no chosen edges,

8
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.760
so each vertex is a

9
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.900
tiny tree by itself. Sort

10
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.720
every edge by cost, from

11
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.980
smallest to largest. We will

12
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.280
accept an edge only when

13
00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.700
it joins two different trees.

14
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.640
Union-find keeps track of which

15
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.800
tree each vertex belongs to.

16
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.440
Find returns that tree's representative,

17
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.600
which is its parent-pointer root.

18
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.840
If two endpoints have different

19
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.100
roots, union merges their groups;

20
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.820
equal roots mean we must

21
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:41.780
reject the edge.

22
00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.000
First consider the edge from

23
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.760
C to E, costing one.

24
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.240
C and E are in

25
00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.300
separate trees, so accept the

26
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.320
edge. Union links root C

27
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.200
under root E; one of

28
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.420
our eight trees has now

29
00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.220
merged with another.

30
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.520
The coloured edges are the

31
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.760
connections we have actually selected.

32
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.260
Grey possibilities are hidden here

33
00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.620
so the separate trees are

34
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:04.460
easy to see.

35
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.880
Next take the edge from

36
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.480
A to B, costing two.

37
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.620
Its endpoints are still separate,

38
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.220
so accept it and link

39
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.980
root A under root B.

40
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.640
Notice that our A and

41
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.960
B tree is separate from

42
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.240
the C and E tree.

43
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.300
These groups do not need

44
00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.640
to touch yet. Kruskal always

45
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.400
checks the next cheapest edge

46
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.540
anywhere in the graph.

47
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.500
The edge from G to

48
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.185
H also costs two. Accept

49
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.660
it and link root G

50
00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.140
under root H. We now

51
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.280
have three little two-vertex trees,

52
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.440
plus the single vertices D

53
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.160
and F.

54
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.200
Five separate trees remain, even

55
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.580
though three edges have been

56
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:45.920
accepted. The next accepted edge

57
00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.400
will reduce that count by

58
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:47.680
one.

59
00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.940
At cost three, we will

60
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.340
try A to C before

61
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.380
the other tied edges. A

62
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:54.540
belongs to the A and

63
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.240
B tree; C belongs to

64
00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.420
the C and E tree.

65
00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.620
They are different trees, so

66
00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:00.880
this edge can join them.

67
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.820
Watch the parent-pointer view. From

68
00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.600
A, follow the arrow to

69
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.280
B; B is its own

70
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.320
parent, so find returns B.

71
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.220
From C, follow the arrow

72
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.740
to E; find returns E.

73
00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.840
Accept the graph edge from

74
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.420
A to C. Union links

75
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.400
root B under root E,

76
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.100
merging both whole groups. The

77
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.720
chosen cable is A to

78
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.420
C, while the new parent

79
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.100
arrow is B to E;

80
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.720
those are different jobs.

81
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.300
The graph now contains one

82
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.360
four-vertex tree with A, B,

83
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.800
C, and E. D is

84
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.460
still alone, and G and

85
00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.060
H still form another tree.

86
00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.740
Parent arrows are bookkeeping; they

87
00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.680
do not have to match

88
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.000
the selected graph edges.

89
00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.920
The edge from A to

90
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.560
D also costs three. Find

91
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.180
A returns E, while find

92
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.860
D returns D, so accept

93
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.100
it. Union puts the lower-rank

94
00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:56.560
root D under E, bringing

95
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.080
D into the larger group.

96
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.040
Next is the edge from

97
00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.100
B to E, again costing

98
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.600
three. Find B and find

99
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.880
E both return E, so

100
00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.700
the endpoints are already connected.

101
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:10.720
The orange path runs from

102
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.140
B through A and C

103
00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:13.720
to E; adding the red

104
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.040
edge would close a cycle,

105
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.160
so reject it.

106
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.640
The edge from B to

107
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.640
C costs four, but these

108
00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.640
vertices are also already in

109
00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.260
the same tree. The existing

110
00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.040
path through A already connects

111
00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.080
them. Reject this edge too;

112
00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.380
neither rejection changes the groups.

113
00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.920
Now consider E to H,

114
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.560
costing four. E represents the

115
00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.960
large tree; H represents the

116
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.210
G and H tree. Accept

117
00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.620
this edge because it joins

118
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:42.280
those two trees without creating

119
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:42.780
a cycle.

120
00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:45.420
Union links the lower-rank root

121
00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.240
H under E. G still

122
00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.080
points to H, so the

123
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.320
whole G and H group

124
00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.460
comes along. We changed just

125
00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.120
one parent pointer to merge

126
00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:54.900
two sets.

127
00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.100
At cost five, try C

128
00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.580
to D first. Both endpoints

129
00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:02.240
already lead to root E,

130
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.920
and the selected path through

131
00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.260
A connects them. Reject this

132
00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.560
edge because it would create

133
00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:08.100
a cycle.

134
00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:10.140
The edge from F to

135
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.740
H also costs five. F

136
00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:14.520
is alone, but H leads

137
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.360
to root E, so accept

138
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.720
the edge and link root

139
00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.920
F under E. We have

140
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.480
accepted seven edges; all eight

141
00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.160
vertices now belong to one

142
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:24.480
tree.

143
00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.120
The accepted costs are one,

144
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.500
two, two, three, three, four,

145
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.960
and five, totalling twenty. We

146
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.780
can stop now, without checking

147
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.960
the more expensive edges. A

148
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.760
connected graph with eight vertices

149
00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.660
needs seven accepted edges.

150
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.240
Why process edges from cheapest

151
00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.520
to most expensive? The cheapest

152
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.720
available edge joining different trees

153
00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.660
is safe by the cut

154
00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.940
property. Union-find only answers whether

155
00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.760
its endpoints are already connected;

156
00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.980
the sorted order supplies the

157
00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:58.900
greedy cost choice.

158
00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.562
Now let us count Kruskal's

159
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.480
work step by step. Let

160
00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:04.960
n be the number of

161
00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.860
vertices and m the number

162
00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.280
of undirected edges: eight and

163
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.300
fourteen in this example. Separate

164
00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.400
preparing the edge list, ordering

165
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.380
it, and checking whether each

166
00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:18.240
candidate joins different groups.

167
00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.060
Adjacency lists are neighbour lists.

168
00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.760
To collect unique edges for

169
00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.720
Kruskal, scan one list per

170
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.360
vertex and two entries per

171
00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.500
edge. This costs the vertex

172
00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.340
count plus the edge count,

173
00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:31.940
shown as order n plus

174
00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:34.380
m; the collected edges still

175
00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.040
need sorting.

176
00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:36.880
If the input is an

177
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.340
adjacency matrix, inspect one triangular

178
00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:41.960
half to avoid duplicate undirected

179
00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.820
edges. There are n times

180
00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.440
n minus one over two

181
00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.880
possible pairs, so preparation costs

182
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.580
order n squared even if

183
00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:51.640
few edges exist. This input

184
00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.240
cost does not disappear when

185
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:54.520
we speed up sorting or

186
00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:54.840
union-find.

187
00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.060
Comparison sorting orders m edge

188
00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:00.520
records, not n vertices. With

189
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.680
merge sort, each level processes

190
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.500
at most order m records,

191
00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.080
and there are about log

192
00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.340
m levels. Multiply the work

193
00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:09.520
per level by the number

194
00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:10.660
of levels to get order

195
00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.360
m log m; this is

196
00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.820
a growth bound, not an

197
00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.320
exact comparison count.

198
00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.540
Our final tree has seven

199
00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.440
edges, but we considered ten

200
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.960
candidates: seven accepted and three

201
00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.640
rejected. Every rejected candidate still

202
00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:26.760
needs a connectivity test. In

203
00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.420
general we may examine all

204
00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:29.940
m edges, so use m

205
00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.260
for the testing bound, not

206
00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.320
n minus one.

207
00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:34.900
Each candidate needs two endpoint

208
00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.100
finds. Reuse those returned roots

209
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.540
for union: our trace then

210
00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.200
has twenty finds and seven

211
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.540
successful root links. A union

212
00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.740
routine that repeats the finds

213
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.200
adds only a constant factor;

214
00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.280
the total number of union-find

215
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.960
operations is still order m.

216
00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.060
The cost of each find

217
00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.340
depends on the group data

218
00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:58.120
structure. Unbalanced parent chains can

219
00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.700
take linear time per find,

220
00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:01.073
giving an order m n

221
00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.600
bound for connectivity work. Union

222
00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.120
by rank limits depth to

223
00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:06.820
log n, so rank alone

224
00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.160
gives order m log n

225
00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.040
for the same work.

226
00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.180
With both union by rank

227
00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.600
and path compression, each operation

228
00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:15.627
costs inverse Ackermann n amortised

229
00:07:15.627 --> 00:07:18.260
across a sequence. Write this

230
00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:19.840
as alpha n; it grows

231
00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:21.480
extremely slowly, but is not

232
00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.140
literally constant. Order m operations

233
00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.300
therefore give order m times

234
00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:28.280
alpha n work, plus order

235
00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.600
n to create the singleton

236
00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:29.900
sets.

237
00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.740
For an edge-list input, now

238
00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:36.340
add initialisation, comparison sorting, and

239
00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:39.140
connectivity work. The full bound

240
00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:40.740
is order n plus m

241
00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:42.840
log m plus m times

242
00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:44.720
alpha n. On a connected

243
00:07:44.693 --> 00:07:48.060
nontrivial graph, sorting usually dominates

244
00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.080
this expression, giving the familiar

245
00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.240
m log m bound; the

246
00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.040
earlier terms have not magically

247
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:54.440
vanished.

248
00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.040
If the edge list is

249
00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.100
already sorted by weight, remove

250
00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.769
only the sorting bill. Initialisation

251
00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.180
and the candidate tests remain,

252
00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.220
giving order n plus m

253
00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:06.420
times alpha n. If the

254
00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:07.760
graph arrives as a matrix,

255
00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.100
add its quadratic preparation bill

256
00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.200
as well; presorted candidates do

257
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.680
not erase matrix scanning.

258
00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.700
A binary heap is an

259
00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.472
alternative way to order Kruskal's

260
00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.480
edge candidates. Build a heap

261
00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:20.540
of m edges in linear

262
00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:22.280
time, then remove the next

263
00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:23.460
cheapest edge at log m

264
00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.100
cost, up to m times.

265
00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:27.280
The worst-case ordering cost is

266
00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.800
still m log m, and

267
00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.320
you still need union-find to

268
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:31.020
test cycles.

269
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.625
For space, union-find stores a

270
00:08:33.625 --> 00:08:35.540
parent and rank per vertex,

271
00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:38.380
using order n memory. The

272
00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:39.740
edge list or edge heap

273
00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:42.000
stores order m records, and

274
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.440
merge sort can need another

275
00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.780
order m buffer. A matrix

276
00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.160
input itself still occupies order

277
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.860
n squared storage; always distinguish

278
00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:52.340
the input from the extra

279
00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:52.740
workspace.

280
00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:54.960
Things to take note of.

281
00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:57.120
Tied edges can be processed

282
00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.000
in either order, which may

283
00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.540
change the chosen tree but

284
00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:03.120
preserves the minimum cost. A

285
00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.420
disconnected graph produces several minimum

286
00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.680
trees, called a minimum spanning

287
00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:08.120
forest.

288
00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.660
Kruskal merges a forest. In

289
00:09:10.660 --> 00:09:13.060
cost order, find both endpoints:

290
00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.180
different roots means accept and

291
00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.000
union; the same root means

292
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.100
skip the cycle. Keep the

293
00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:20.380
cable drawing and the parent-pointer

